{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>A woman when she produces seed and gives birth.</b> Once she gives birth from the proper place for birth — excluding one born through the abdominal wall. Rabbi Shimon says: one born through the abdominal wall is like a born child, and they are obligated to bring a sacrifice for him, except that he is exempt from the five shekels of a firstborn son." "<b>And gives birth.</b> Even an eight-month-old, a seven-month-old, a six-month-old, a five-month-old." "<b>Male.</b> To include if she gave birth to a dead one." "<b>And gives birth a male.</b> It is found in the Book of Generations: a woman has seven orifices — three on the right and three on the left, and one in the middle. When the seed enters through those on the right, she gives birth to a male; if it enters through those on the left, she gives birth to a female; and if it enters through the middle one, she gives birth to a tumtum or an androgyne. When she lies immediately after intercourse on her right side, the seed enters through those orifices on the right and she gives birth to a male, and the impurity hastens to depart — therefore she is impure only seven days and pure thirty-three days. And when she lies immediately after intercourse on her left side, the seed enters through the orifices on the left and she gives birth to a female, and the impurity does not hasten to depart — therefore she is impure two weeks. And this is what is meant by ״His left hand is under my head and His right hand embraces me״ — in order to give birth to males." "<b>And she is impure.</b> She is impure, but the child is not impure." "<b>Seven days.</b> Count seven days from the last birth. The disciples asked Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai: why did the Torah say for a male seven and for a female fourteen days? He said to them: a male, since everyone rejoices over him, the mother renounces her vow for seven days; a female, since everyone is grieved over her, she renounces her for fourteen days." "<b>Nidat.</b> A term of distancing, as in ״ומתבול ינידוהו״ (and being Reproach they would Wave Him Away); meaning, she is driven away and distanced from her husband." "<b>As the days of the nidat of her infirmity.</b> It is taught: Rabbi Meir said: why did the Torah say she shall be a nidah for seven? Since he is accustomed to her, he becomes satiated with her; the Torah said she shall be a nidah for seven, so that she will be beloved to her husband on the day of her purification as on the day of her entry to the canopy."], :book :leviticus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:11:43.594994356Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}